<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    <p class="lede">Written for: a U.S. citizen, California resident, ages 19&ndash;64 (assumed), with $0 earned income &mdash; money comes from Kalshi trading contracts, rental property, and savings interest, and the person works as a self-directed property manager and full-time trader.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="bottom-h">
    <h3 id="bottom-h">The bottom line</h3>
    <div class="panel accent">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>You do not need earned income to obtain health coverage in California.</strong> Both Medi-Cal and Covered California set eligibility on household MAGI, and MAGI already counts your rental, interest, and trading income &mdash; it does not require wages. (<a href="https://www.healthcare.gov/income-and-household-information/income/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healthcare.gov income rules</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Today, Medi-Cal has no work requirement at all.</strong> If your household MAGI is at or below 138% FPL ($22,025/year for one person in 2026), you can enroll in Medi-Cal with no premium, no asset test, and no work test &mdash; and keep it through December 31, 2026 on exactly those terms. (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/updates/medi-cal-changes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS Medi-Cal Changes</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>From January 1, 2027, a federal work and community engagement rule applies to Medi-Cal expansion adults &mdash; but it is an hours-or-income rule, not an earned-income rule, and in California it is not an every-month test.</strong> California requires engagement for <strong>any one month</strong> since your last determination (new applicants: the month right before applying). You can satisfy that qualifying month with zero earned income by: <strong>MAGI-based monthly income of at least $580 from any source</strong> (net rental + interest + trading gains count); <strong>80 hours of structured community service</strong>; <strong>at least half-time school</strong>; a work program; any combination totaling 80 hours; or a qualifying exemption. (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-435/section-435.552" target="_blank" rel="noopener">42 CFR 435.552</a>; <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB2161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AB 2161, Welf. &amp; Inst. Code 14005.69(d)&ndash;(e)</a>; <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MEDIL-I26-25.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS MEDIL I26-25</a>) <strong>Warning:</strong> if you lose Medi-Cal for noncompliance, DHCS says you <strong>cannot</strong> get financial help through Covered California &mdash; full-cost plans only &mdash; so meeting the rule matters more than treating the marketplace as a fallback.</li>
        <li><strong>If your MAGI is above 138% FPL, Covered California has no work requirement whatsoever.</strong> Federal premium tax credits cover households 100&ndash;400% FPL, California adds state subsidies up to 200% FPL for 2027, and Silver cost-sharing reductions help up to 250% FPL. Open enrollment runs November 1, 2026 &ndash; January 31, 2027. (<a href="https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/premium-tax-credit/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healthcare.gov</a>; <a href="https://www.coveredca.com/press-releases/2026/07/21/covered-california-rates-and-plans-for-2027-california-continues-fight-for-health-insurance-affordability-and-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Your income streams all count toward MAGI, and none of them is "earned income" &mdash; that combination is coherent, not a loophole.</strong> Interest, capital gains (including Kalshi trading results), and net rental income are unearned, yet all counted for Medi-Cal and premium tax credits; the $580 work-rule standard is itself measured with <strong>MAGI-based income</strong>, so the same unearned dollars can satisfy it. IRS rules confirm why earned income stays $0: own-account trading gains are not self-employment earnings even for a full-time qualifying trader, and rental real-estate income is generally not SE earnings either. (If you are actually paid fees to manage <em>other people's</em> property, that is earned business income &mdash; a different fact pattern; report it.) (<a href="https://healthcare.gov/income-and-household-information/income/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healthcare.gov</a>; <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-435/section-435.552" target="_blank" rel="noopener">42 CFR 435.552(f)</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc429" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Topic 429</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040se" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Schedule E instructions</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>California added protections.</strong> The state statute is Welf. &amp; Inst. Code &sect;14005.69, added by <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB164" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SB 164</a> (Chapter 27, Statutes of 2026, effective June 29, 2026) and amended by AB 2161 (Chapter 209, Statutes of 2026, chaptered September 14, 2026). It requires counties to verify from existing data before demanding paperwork, to keep Medi-Cal active during the 30-day noncompliance response window, and to check every other basis of Medi-Cal eligibility before denying or terminating anyone. (<a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=WIC&amp;sectionNum=14005.69" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leginfo history note and text</a>; <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB2161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AB 2161 bill text</a>)</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="doors-h">
    <h3 id="doors-h">Your decision paths</h3>
    <p>Which door applies depends on one number &mdash; your household MAGI for the coverage year (tax filer + spouse + tax dependents; see the <a href="income-treatment.html">Income &amp; MAGI</a> page). Note that trading losses, depreciation, and rental losses can lower MAGI, so the number can move year to year.</p>
    {{TABLE:options-summary.csv::Decision paths for a California resident with $0 earned income}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="law-h">
    <h3 id="law-h">The new law in plain English</h3>
    <div class="panel blue">
      <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The One Big Beautiful Bill Act (H.R. 1, Public Law 119-21, signed July 4, 2025) added a &ldquo;community engagement requirement&rdquo; to Medicaid via <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1/text" target="_blank" rel="noopener">section 71119</a> (new section 1902(xx) of the Social Security Act). CMS turned it into an operating rule on June 1, 2026 (<a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/06/03/2026-11094/medicaid-program-community-engagement-requirement-for-certain-individuals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS-2454-IFC</a>, 91 FR 33348, effective July 31, 2026).</p>
      <p><strong>Who it covers.</strong> Non-pregnant adults 19&ndash;64, not on Medicare, who get Medicaid through the ACA expansion group (in California, the MAGI &ldquo;New Adult Group&rdquo;). People with MAGI above Medi-Cal limits are not in this group at all &mdash; they use Covered California, which has no work requirement.</p>
      <p><strong>What it requires.</strong> For a <em>qualifying month</em>: 80 hours of work, community service, or a work program; or at least half-time school; or any combination totaling 80 hours; or MAGI-based monthly income of at least 80 &times; the federal minimum wage ($580 in 2026). <strong>California timing (AB 2161):</strong> new applicants must show a compliant month immediately before the application month; enrolled members must show compliance in <em>any one month</em> between determinations &mdash; checked at renewal (every six months under section 71107), not every month and not when you merely report a change. Noncompliance triggers a notice with 30 calendar days to respond while coverage continues, followed by an all-other-eligibility check and hearing rights before denial/disenrollment; reapplication is allowed at any time (California preliminary guidance also allows return without a new application within a 90-day cure period after discontinuance once compliance is shown).</p>
      <p><strong>Exemptions.</strong> Parents/caregivers of a child 13 or younger (or of a disabled person), pregnancy and 12-month postpartum, disability, medical frailty and serious health conditions, American Indians/Alaska Natives, totally disabled veterans, former foster youth under 26, people meeting CalWORKs/TANF rules, CalFresh households subject to SNAP work rules, people in substance-use treatment, and inmates. States may add short-term hardship exceptions (hospitalization, medical travel, high-unemployment or disaster counties).</p>
      <p><strong>Cost.</strong> CBO (via <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48755" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CRS R48755</a>) estimates the provision cuts federal Medicaid spending by $325.6 billion over FY2025&ndash;2034 and leaves an average of 4.5 million more people uninsured per year &mdash; overwhelmingly through paperwork, not actual ineligibility.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fulfill-h">
    <h3 id="fulfill-h">Exactly how you can fulfill the work requirement with $0 earned income</h3>
    <div class="panel accent">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The income standard (no hours needed) &mdash; the most direct route on your facts.</strong> In a qualifying month, <strong>MAGI-based monthly income of at least $580</strong> satisfies the rule outright. The measure is MAGI (42 CFR 435.552(f)(2); Welf. &amp; Inst. Code 14005.69(e)(6)): <em>net</em> rental income + interest (including tax-exempt added back) + <em>net</em> capital/trading gains &mdash; not gross rents, not gross trade proceeds, and not wages only. DHCS's member letter says the same thing in plain language: &ldquo;income from any source.&rdquo; At renewal you need only <em>one</em> such month since your last determination, so a single month where rent + interest + realized gains clear $580 on a MAGI basis carries the renewal even if other months are thin or negative. Six-month averaging exists federally only for <em>seasonal workers</em>; DHCS wording sounds broader, so do not count on averaging variable trading gains without county confirmation &mdash; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a> (FL-06, FL-11).</li>
        <li><strong>Structured community service.</strong> 80 hours in a qualifying month of community service counts on its own &mdash; no earnings required &mdash; but it must be unpaid work through a <strong>structured program of a public or nonprofit organization that oversees it, tracks dates/hours, and can confirm them</strong> (and not for a partisan purpose). Informal, untracked volunteering may not count; get the host's tracking/confirmation process in writing first.</li>
        <li><strong>School.</strong> Enrollment in an educational program at least half-time satisfies the rule by itself.</li>
        <li><strong>A work program.</strong> 80 hours/month in a job-training program counts.</li>
        <li><strong>Any combination</strong> of work, community service, work program, and less-than-half-time education totaling 80 hours in the qualifying month (education hours use a federal formula: credit hours &times; 3 &times; 4.33 weeks). A federal <em>state option</em> also lets the agency convert MAGI-based income below $580 into work hours (income &divide; $7.25) when hours are undocumented &mdash; California's use of that option is unconfirmed, so ask before relying on it.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-employment counts as work in general</strong> &mdash; federal &ldquo;work&rdquo; covers paid, in-kind, and unpaid work, the CMS preamble includes people who work for themselves (business owners, independent contractors), and DHCS county guidance lists self-employment (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-435/section-435.552" target="_blank" rel="noopener">42 CFR 435.552(b)</a>) &mdash; <strong>but neither of your two activities has an official yes.</strong> Whether hours trading your own Kalshi account, or hours managing your <em>own</em> rentals, qualify is <em>not addressed</em> anywhere official and is flagged (FL-02, FL-12). Tax law cuts both ways: it confirms those profits are not self-employment <em>earnings</em>, without defining Medicaid <em>work</em>. Do not rely on trading or self-management hours without written county confirmation; use the verified routes above. (Paid work managing <em>others'</em> property is ordinary self-employment &mdash; but then you would have earned income to report.)</li>
        <li><strong>Exemptions.</strong> If any exemption applies to your situation (caregiver of a child 13 or younger, medical condition, etc.), no activity is required at all.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ca-h">
    <h3 id="ca-h">What California is doing for people with $0 earned income</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Citizen or green card? The 2027 rules diverge.</strong> DHCS&rsquo;s immigration-status table lists U.S. citizens (including naturalized citizens) as keeping full Medi-Cal and full dental and paying no premium. Lawful permanent residents still inside the five-year waiting period move to fee-for-service in January 2027, and the same table lists a July 2027 step for LPRs age 21&ndash;59 who are not pregnant: &ldquo;Must pay premium to keep Medi-Cal.&rdquo; This brief assumes the U.S. citizenship stated in the request; if &ldquo;permanent resident&rdquo; means a green card, that row applies. (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/immigration-status-and-changes-to-medi-cal-eligibility/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS immigration-status table</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>No work rule until 2027.</strong> Medi-Cal eligibility through the end of 2026 is unchanged: income-based, no asset test for MAGI groups, no work test.</li>
      <li><strong>AB 2161 safeguards</strong> (Chapter 209, Statutes of 2026, signed September 14, 2026): legislative intent that the rule be administered in the way &ldquo;least administratively burdensome&rdquo; to members; data-first verification using wage records, Medi-Cal aid codes, claims and encounter data, and Department of Social Services data; 30 calendar days that start when you actually receive the notice (deemed received five days after its date); Medi-Cal services continue during that window; active eligibility maintained for renewing members until at least the last day of the month of the 30-day period; screening for every other Medi-Cal or insurance-affordability basis before any denial; a duty to have you confirm information when reliable data sources conflict before any adverse action (subdivision (i)); written notice and a fair-hearing right; and self-attestation that you never received a timely notice counts as good cause. Separately, DHCS county guidance says counties do not reassess the requirement when you report a change in circumstance. (<a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB2161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AB 2161 bill text</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>No state work requirement of its own.</strong> California's statute implements the federal minimum and sunsets with it (Welf. &amp; Inst. Code &sect;14005.69; the AB 2161 text locates the sunset in subdivision (p), which the current code display does not show &mdash; FL-29); CRS confirms that before P.L. 119-21 there was no statutory Medicaid work requirement at all. Implementing regulations are due by July 1, 2029, and implementation is gated on the director's systems certification and on continued federal financial participation.</li>
      <li><strong>Free help to comply.</strong> DHCS says &ldquo;California lawmakers have invested in new resources to help you with these new rules&rdquo; and promises information on paid work, volunteering, job training, and schools before the end of 2026; help is available from the county Medi-Cal office, the DHCS work-rules page, the Medi-Cal Helpline at (800) 541-5555, and BenefitsCal. (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MEDIL-I26-25.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS MEDIL I26-25</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>State subsidy expansion:</strong> $300 million appropriated for 2027 (up from $190 million), extending state premium help to households up to 200% FPL &mdash; about 30% of Covered California enrollees, with nearly 200,000 able to pick a Silver plan at a $0 premium. (<a href="https://www.coveredca.com/press-releases/2026/07/21/covered-california-rates-and-plans-for-2027-california-continues-fight-for-health-insurance-affordability-and-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California, July 21, 2026</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>No premiums for Medi-Cal.</strong> Medi-Cal coverage carries no monthly premium; the only member charges DHCS lists are the October 1, 2028 copayments (above $0 and up to $35 per service, capped at 5% of household income, with exemptions for emergency, primary, prenatal, pediatric, family-planning, FQHC and rural clinic, and behavioral-health services) and July 1, 2027 premiums tied to specific immigration-status groups.</li>
      <li><strong>You only need one qualifying month per renewal</strong> &mdash; and the months need not be consecutive. A 90-day cure period applies after coverage ends, and reapplication is allowed at any time. (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/25-30.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS ACWDL 25-30</a>)</li>
      <li><strong>Outreach:</strong> DHCS texted members from February 2026 and began mailing plain-language notices in late August 2026; counties must include them with applications from October 1 through December 31, 2026.</li>
      <li><strong>Projections:</strong> DHCS's February 2026 stakeholder deck estimates up to 233,000 members could lose coverage by June 2027 and about 1.4 million by June 2028; the 2026-27 Budget Act projects 44,000 disenrollments in 2026-27 and 1.3 million by 2029-30. Both are DHCS estimates from different dates &mdash; mostly paperwork failures, which is why responding to county letters quickly matters.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="action-h">
    <h3 id="action-h">Action checklist</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ol>
        <li>Estimate this year's MAGI: net rental income + interest + net trading gains (minus allowed capital losses) for the whole tax household. Use the <a href="https://www.healthcare.gov/income-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">healthcare.gov income calculator</a>.</li>
        <li>If MAGI &le; $22,025 (one person): <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/apply/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">apply for Medi-Cal</a> now &mdash; online (BenefitsCal or Covered California), by mail, phone, or in person. Keep your contact information current with the county.</li>
        <li>Open and answer every Medi-Cal/county letter immediately; from March 2027 renewals come every six months for expansion adults.</li>
        <li>Before January 2027, pick your qualifying-month path and keep <em>monthly</em> proof by source: Kalshi monthly P&amp;L + realized gain/loss records, rent ledger showing rents received <em>and</em> expenses (MAGI uses net), bank interest statements, structured-volunteer hour logs with a named contact, or school enrollment records. Remember California needs only one compliant month per renewal period &mdash; but at application it must be the month right before you apply, so time a 2027 application accordingly. Do not assume that failing the rule lets you switch to a <em>subsidized</em> Covered California plan: DHCS says financial help is unavailable after a noncompliance loss.</li>
        <li>If MAGI is 138&ndash;400% FPL: shop at <a href="https://www.coveredca.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California</a> during open enrollment (November 1, 2026 &ndash; January 31, 2027); report income changes during the year to avoid repaying excess advance credits (caps were removed for tax years after 2025).</li>
        <li>For the Kalshi tax questions (section 1256 vs. other treatment), get a tax professional &mdash; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="unknowns-h">
    <h3 id="unknowns-h">Assumptions, unknowns, and flags</h3>
    <div class="panel warn">
      <p>This research states rules; it does not determine your eligibility. Age, household size, county, actual MAGI, and tax filings are unknown and were not assumed. Values that could not be confirmed from an official source are left blank and flagged, and several inconsistencies between official pages are documented. See the <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a> page for the complete list.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
